SENDER DISAMBIGUATION
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# "emails from X" where X is a person's name — sender vs account disambiguation
USER: "roberto's emails"
   ↑ "from X" indicates SENDER, not an account named X. Search all accounts for those sent by X.
[step 1] read_messages(account="all", from_contains="roberto")
[step 2] describe_entries(from_step=1, style="by_importance")
final_answer: "You have N emails sent by or regarding 'roberto': ..."

══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
ANTI-COLLISION (4/5/2026, ADR 0079)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

MUST: call the executor that already exists in your pool when its EXACT name is present.
MUST: use a top-K executor if its `affinity` (visible in description) overlaps at least 1 keyword with the query. `request_new_executor` is LAST RESORT: only when NO top-K tool matches semantically.
MUST NOT: call `request_new_executor` with an `expected_name` that is already in the pool, nor propose `list_X`/`find_X`/`read_X` when `get_X` already exists (or vice versa) for the same object: the 5 producer-verbs are ORTHOGONAL (CLAUDE.md §2.2).
MUST NOT: call `request_new_executor` when a top-K tool covers the query: the runtime has a jaccard soft-gate that rejects the request and redirects you to the existing tool (live case "appointments tomorrow" → read_events).
OK: pool=[get_processes,...], query "list running processes" → get_processes().
OK: pool=[get_now, read_events, compute_entries], query "appointments tomorrow" → read_events (affinity matches).
ERROR: pool=[get_processes,...], query "list running processes" → request_new_executor(expected_name="list_processes"). THIS IS AN ERROR.
ERROR: pool=[..., read_events, ...], query "appointments tomorrow" → request_new_executor(expected_name="get_appointments"). THIS IS AN ERROR (read_events covers the intent).

══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
EXECUTOR > FAST-PATH (19/5/2026 v5, ADR 0150)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

MUST: ALWAYS prefer a catalog executor over a fast-path pipeline that does the same thing. If the pool contains a synthesised executor that wraps a sequence, use it directly in ONE step.
MUST NOT: emit the multi-step sequence `[get_X, describe_entries]` when `describe_X` (a synthesised executor from L2→L3 promotion) is present in the pool. The L2 fast-path is auto-demoted by the runtime in this case; you, in parallel, must avoid proposing it.
OK: pool=[describe_urls, get_urls, describe_entries], query "download and describe this page" → describe_urls(url=...). One step.
ERROR: pool=[describe_urls, get_urls, describe_entries], query "download and describe this page" → [step 1] get_urls(...) [step 2] describe_entries(from_step=1). THIS IS AN ERROR (describe_urls does both in one shot).

══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
IMPLICIT ACTIONS (14/5/2026, ADR 0129)
══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

MUST: for every entry in `intent.implicit_actions` with `strategy="auto"`, EMIT the step `<entry.verb>(...)` AFTER the input-gathering steps (find_/get_inputs) and BEFORE the notify verb (send_messages). Args derived from scratchpad (e.g. {% raw %}`{{stepN.values.choice}}`{% endraw %} for user pick) + plausible domain defaults.
MUST: for `strategy="ask"`, EMIT `get_inputs(kind="yes_no", title="Confirm action", dialog=[{var:"confirm", prompt:"Also <entry.verb>?", schema:{kind:"yes_no"}}])` BEFORE the implicit mutating step. Execute `<entry.verb>` ONLY if `values.confirm==true`; otherwise skip straight to notify.
MUST NOT: ignore `implicit_actions`, reorder them BEFORE the choice get_inputs, or substitute them with send_messages (the email would confirm a state that does not exist).
OK: «propose appointment next week + email me» → intent.implicit_actions=[{verb:"create_events",strategy:"ask"}] → find_events_empty → get_inputs(choice) → get_inputs(yes_no confirm) → create_events(summary,start,end) → send_messages → final_answer.
ERROR: skipping `create_events` when implicit_actions[0].strategy in ("auto","ask"+confirm=true). The email confirms a state that does not exist in the calendar.

